Scientists think Venus may once have been cooler, perhaps even with shallow oceans, though that is still debated. Being closer to the Sun, it got warmer. Water evaporated, and water vapour traps heat too, so it warmed even more. Eventually the oceans boiled away completely.

Carbon dioxide that on Earth is locked up in rocks and seawater ended up in Venus's air. The result is a planet that can't cool down. Earth has a greenhouse effect too, and a gentle one keeps us warm enough to live. Burning fuels adds [[@carbon-cycle|carbon dioxide]] to our air, which is why scientists study Venus to understand [[@climate-change|climate change]].

{{story:climate-change}}

Long before film, people noticed something odd about dark rooms. Light coming through a small hole paints an upside-down picture of the outside world on the far wall. This is called a *camera obscura*, Latin for "dark room". Around the year 1000, the scientist Ibn al-Haytham, working in Cairo, described how it worked in his *Book of Optics*.

Your own eye works the same way. The pupil is the hole, the lens focuses the light, and the [[retina|retina]] at the back catches the picture. Your brain quietly turns it the right way up.

{{story:eyes-see}}

The bubbles come from a detergent, often sodium lauryl sulfate, which is a relative of the cleaners in shampoo and [[@soap-history|soap]]. Foam doesn't clean teeth much on its own. It spreads the paste around the mouth and makes brushing feel like it is working, which helps people keep going.

Sweeteners such as sorbitol make toothpaste taste nice without feeding the germs that cause [[@tooth-decay|tooth decay]]. Those germs love sugar. Sorbitol and similar sweeteners give them almost nothing to eat.

{{story:teeth}}

Turbine blades aren't flat paddles. Each one is curved like the wing of an [[@airplanes-fly|aeroplane]]. As wind flows over it, the curved shape creates a force called lift, which pulls the blade sideways and keeps the [[rotor|rotor]] turning. The blades are twisted along their length, because the tip moves much faster than the part near the hub.

Big land turbines often have blades 50 to 80 metres long. The tip of such a blade can race through the air at more than 250 km/h, faster than most express trains, even though the rotor seems to turn lazily. A small motor swivels the whole top so the blades always face into the wind.

{{story:wind-blows}}

The traditional "paint" is made from things a farming family already has. Walls are smoothed with a mix of mud and cow dung, which dries to an earthy brown. The white comes from rice ground into a paste with water and a little gum to help it stick.

The brush was often a [[@bamboo|bamboo]] twig, chewed at one end until it frayed. That gave the painter thin, slightly uneven lines, which is part of why Warli figures look so lively. Because mud walls get repaired and replastered, the paintings were never meant to last forever. They were renewed for each special occasion.

{{story:symmetry}}

In India, the outpatient department, or OPD, is where people come without staying overnight. In big government hospitals the queues can start before sunrise. A doctor there may have only a few minutes for each patient. They have to listen fast, think fast and still be kind.

In the US, many people see a family doctor in a small clinic by appointment, often for 15 to 20 minutes. In both countries, the doctor's first tool is the question "What brings you here today?"

{{story:antibiotics}}

Parks helped save animals that were disappearing. In the USA, Yellowstone kept the last wild herd of [[@bison-return|bison]]. In 1995, wolves were brought back to the park after about 70 years away, and they changed how elk, trees and rivers behaved.

In India, Project Tiger began in 1973 with nine tiger reserves. There are now more than 50. India's 2022 tiger count estimated about 3,700 wild [[@tiger-stripes|tigers]], roughly three-quarters of all the wild tigers in the world.

{{story:yellowstone-wolves}}

India is still a young country. In 2021, only about one person in ten was 60 or older. But that share is growing fast, because people live longer and families have fewer children. A report by the UN Population Fund in 2023 projected that by 2050, about one in five Indians will be elderly. That's around 35 crore people, roughly the whole population of the US today.

Some states are ageing faster than others. Kerala and Tamil Nadu already have a much higher share of older people than Bihar or Uttar Pradesh, where families are larger.

{{story:population}}

On 20 November 1989, the United Nations adopted the Convention on the Rights of the Child. It has 54 articles. That day is now celebrated as World Children's Day.

The treaty rests on four main ideas. No child should be treated unfairly. Adults should do what is best for children. Every child has the right to life and to grow up well. And children should be listened to. India joined in 1992. Today 196 countries have joined, more than for any other [[@human-rights|human rights]] treaty. The United States signed in 1995, but has not ratified it.

{{story:mid-day-meal}}

Take a strip of squares two high and a few long, and cover it with dominoes, each covering two squares. A strip one long can be covered in only one way. Two long, in two ways. Three long, three ways. Four long, five ways. Five long, eight ways.

Each answer is the sum of the two before it. That is the Fibonacci sequence, which also turns up in [[@fibonacci-nature|sunflower seeds and pine cones]]. The reason is neat. The right-hand end of the strip is either one upright domino or two lying flat, so every tiling grows out of the two shorter strips.

{{story:video-game-history}}

Without the offside rule, a striker could stand next to the other team's goalkeeper and wait for a long ball. So the rule says that when a teammate passes to you, you must not be nearer the goal line than the second-last defender. Usually the last defender is the goalkeeper. Being level counts as onside, and a player standing in an offside position isn't punished unless they get involved in the play.

It sounds tricky, but it shapes the whole game. Defenders step forward together to catch strikers offside. Strikers time their runs to stay level until the pass is made. Since 2018, World Cups have used the video assistant referee (VAR) to check close calls, sometimes with lines drawn on the replay to the nearest centimetre.

{{story:umpires-referees}}

The Chicago Bulls picked him third in the 1984 draft. That year he also won [[@olympics|Olympic]] gold in Los Angeles. In 1985 Nike launched his shoe, the Air Jordan. At first the NBA objected to its red-and-black colours, and the story of Jordan being fined for wearing it became part of the shoe's legend.

People called him "Air Jordan" because of his leaps. He seemed to hang in the air, though [[@gravity-fall|gravity]] pulls on everyone the same way. His jumps simply lasted a little longer, and he could change hands or twist his body before landing.

{{story:sneakers}}

In [[@ancient-egypt|ancient Egypt]], the favourite was senet, a board of 30 squares played about 5,000 years ago. At first it was a simple race game. Later, Egyptians saw it as a journey through the afterlife, and they buried boards with the dead. The young pharaoh Tutankhamun had several in his tomb, including a beautiful one of ebony and ivory.

Egyptian children also had balls made of leather or reeds, wooden toy animals with moving jaws and spinning tops.

{{story:chess-india}}

दाँतों के लिए सबसे बुरी बात यह नहीं कि चीनी कितनी खाई, बल्कि यह कि कितनी बार खाई। हर मीठे नाश्ते के बाद मुँह कुछ देर तक तेज़ाबी रहता है, अक्सर आधा घंटा या उससे ज़्यादा। फिर लार उसे वापस सामान्य कर देती है।

मीठी ड्रिंक धीरे-धीरे चुस्की लेकर पीना, या एक के बाद एक [[@sweets-world|टॉफ़ियाँ]] चूसना, तेज़ाब को दिन भर आता रखता है। खाने के साथ मिठाई खा लेना और फिर पानी से कुल्ला कर लेना दाँतों के लिए कहीं बेहतर है। टॉफ़ी और किशमिश जैसी चिपचिपी चीज़ें दाँतों से चिपकी रहती हैं और बैक्टीरिया को देर तक खिलाती हैं। चिप्स और बिस्किट जैसे स्टार्च वाले नाश्ते भी यही करते हैं, क्योंकि ये मुँह में टूटकर शक्कर बन जाते हैं।

{{story:neem}}

In the early 1980s, an American engineer named Chuck Hull was using [[ultraviolet|ultraviolet]] light to harden coatings on tables. He realised he could harden a liquid resin one thin layer at a time and build up a shape. He called it stereolithography and was granted a patent in 1986.

For years, 3D printers were large and expensive, used mainly by companies to test designs. When some early [[@patents|patents]] ran out around 2009, cheaper machines appeared. Now [[@future-schools|many schools]] have one. Some printers cost less than a good bicycle. Students use them to print parts for science projects, models of [[molecule|molecules]] and replacement pieces for board games.

{{story:plastic-made}}

In 2010, a lithium-ion battery pack cost more than $1,000 for each kilowatt-hour it could store. By 2025, according to BloombergNEF, the average had fallen to about $108. Factories got bigger, designs got better and chemists found cheaper recipes, such as batteries made with iron and phosphate instead of costly cobalt.

That price drop is a big reason electric cars and scooters have spread so quickly. It also helps countries like India store [[@solar-india|solar power]] made during the day for use at night.

{{story:salt}}

In August 2013, Dutch scientist Mark Post cooked the first lab-grown beef burger on live television in London. It had taken years of work and about 250,000 euros, then roughly 2 crore rupees. Two food critics tasted it. They said it was a bit dry but clearly meat.

Prices have dropped hugely since then, but lab-grown meat still costs far more than meat from a farm. In 2020 a restaurant in Singapore served the first lab-grown chicken bites that customers could buy. In 2023, two US companies were allowed to sell lab-grown chicken. Israel approved lab-grown beef in 2024, and Australia approved a lab-grown quail meat in 2025.

{{story:body-cells}}

The first home console was the Magnavox Odyssey, sold in the USA in 1972. Its inventor, Ralph Baer, had built a test version known as the Brown Box. The Odyssey showed only a few white squares and lines. To add colour, players stuck plastic overlays onto their TV screens.

Then came the Atari 2600 in 1977 and the Nintendo Entertainment System in the 1980s, with games like Super Mario Bros. Each new generation packed more power into the same box under the TV.

{{story:video-game-history}}

A bank keeps only a small part of your money in its vaults. It lends the rest. That works as long as people don't all want their money back on the same day. In the early 1930s, frightened people did exactly that. Thousands of [[@money-banks|banks]] could not pay and shut their doors, wiping out families' savings.

With less money around, people bought less. Shops sold less, so factories made less and fired workers, who then bought even less. By 1933 about a quarter of American workers were jobless. Some lived in shacks made of crates and tin, in camps that people mockingly named "Hoovervilles", after President Herbert Hoover.

{{story:droughts}}

The great hall was the centre of castle life. The lord and his family sat at a raised table at one end, while everyone else ate at long trestle tables.

Meals could be big: [[@wheat-bread|bread]], stews, roast meat on feast days, and fish on the many days when the Church forbade meat. People often used thick slices of stale bread, called trenchers, as plates. Musicians and jugglers entertained guests. At night, servants might sleep on the hall floor near the fire.

{{story:toilets-sewers}}

Neither side trusted the other. John asked Pope Innocent III to cancel the charter, and in August 1215 the Pope declared it invalid. Civil war broke out, and some barons even invited a French prince to take the English throne.

Then, in October 1216, John died. Advisers for his nine-year-old son, Henry III, reissued a revised Magna Carta to win the barons back. It was reissued again in 1217 and 1225. The 1225 version became part of English law and was confirmed many times by later kings.

{{story:latin-roots}}

The biggest danger is [[@altitude|altitude]]. At the top, each breath holds only about half as much oxygen as at sea level. Climbers who go up too fast can get headaches and sickness. In rare cases it becomes life-threatening. Guides tell everyone "pole pole", Swahili for "slowly, slowly".

{{story:altitude}}

The first recorded climb to the summit was in 1889, by the German geographer Hans Meyer and the Austrian climber Ludwig Purtscheller. They were helped by a young local guide, Yohani Lauwo, and other porters whose names were mostly not written down. Today, thousands of Tanzanian porters carry tents, food and water for climbers. A typical group of tourists may have three or four helpers for every climber, and the porters often reach camp first, with tents already up.

A coconut is the seed of the coconut palm, wrapped in a thick, fibrous husk. The husk is full of air, so the coconut floats. Inside, the hard shell protects the seed, and the water and flesh feed the young plant when it sprouts.

This design lets coconuts drift on [[@ocean-currents|ocean currents]] for weeks and still grow when they wash up on a [[@beaches|beach]]. That's one reason coconut palms line [[tropical|tropical]] shores from India to [[@hawaii-islands|Hawaii]]. Scientists think people also spread coconuts, carrying them on long sea voyages as food and water.

{{story:ocean-currents}}
